Example demonstrating RVO/NRVO:

```c++
struct MyBigObject {
  int data[10000];
  MyBigObject() { /* Constructor */ }
  MyBigObject(const MyBigObject& other) { /* Expensive copy constructor */ }
  ~MyBigObject() { /* Destructor */ }
};

MyBigObject createObject() {
  MyBigObject obj;
  // ... initialize obj ...
  return obj; // RVO likely here
}

int main() {
  MyBigObject myObj = createObject(); // NRVO likely here
  return 0;
}
```

**Important Note:**  The compiler is *not* obligated to perform RVO/NRVO.  While highly desirable and common with modern compilers at higher optimization levels (-O2 or -O3 for GCC/Clang, /O2 or /Ox for MSVC), relying on it can lead to unpredictable performance if the optimization is not performed. Explicitly using `std::move` can sometimes help guide the compiler in applying RVO/NRVO, but this isn't a guarantee.
